# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Promoting Components

When a widget stops belonging to one screen and becomes part of the design system.

Read by `/promote-component`, which is read-only until you confirm — and whose most
common correct answer is "this already exists, use that one".

---

## 1. The test

<!-- DECIDE: the bar for promotion. A concrete rule beats a judgement call, because
     a judgement call means every contributor sets their own bar.

     A defensible starting point: two independent screens need it, and the second
     need was not engineered to justify the first. -->

## 2. What promotion requires

Promotion is not a file move. All of these, or it is not promoted:

- [ ] Lives in the shared component directory
- [ ] Takes no feature-specific types in its public API
- [ ] Documented in the design document, by class name
- [ ] Listed in the contributing document's lookup table, so the next person finds
      it instead of rebuilding it
- [ ] Both original call sites migrated to it — leaving one behind means the
      duplicate survives and diverges

## 3. What must not be promoted

<!-- DECIDE: the categories that look shared but are not. Usually: anything holding
     feature state, anything that fetches data, and anything whose "reuse" is two
     screens that happen to look similar today for unrelated reasons. -->

## 4. Layering rule

A shared component must not import from a feature. This is not style — a shared
layer that depends on its consumer cannot be extracted, and is a layering inversion
regardless of extraction plans.

<!-- DERIVED: the project's own rule, from .design-suite/arch-rules.json, which is
     what the layer-boundary audit enforces. -->

## 5. Deprecating instead of promoting

Sometimes the right answer is that the older of two similar components should go.

